mpty geometries can be 3D, empty collections are always 2D\n    50\u2192\n    51\u2192    Parameters\n    52\u2192    ----------\n    53\u2192    geometry : Geometry or array_like\n    54\u2192        Geometry or geometries to convert to WKT.\n    55\u2192    rounding_precision : int, default 6\n    56\u2192        The rounding precision when writing the WKT string. Set to a value of\n    57\u2192        -1 to indicate the full precision.\n    58\u2192    trim : bool, default True\n    59\u2192        If True, trim unnecessary decimals (trailing zeros).\n    60\u2192    output_dimension : int, default None\n    61\u2192        The output dimension for the WKT string. Supported values are 2, 3 and\n    62\u2192        4 for GEOS 3.12+. Default None will automatically choose 3 or 4,\n    63\u2192        depending on the version of GEOS.\n    64\u2192        Specifying 3 means that up to 3 dimensions will be written but 2D\n    65\u2192        geometries will still be represented as 2D in the WKT string.\n    66\u2192    old_3d : bool, default False\n    67\u2192        Enable old style 3D/4D WKT generation. By default, new style 3D/4D WKT\n    68\u2192        (ie. \"POINT Z (10 20 30)\") is returned, but with ``old_3d=True``\n    69\u2192        the WKT will be formatted in the style \"POINT (10 20 30)\".\n    70\u2192    **kwargs\n    71\u2192        See :ref:`NumPy ufunc docs <ufuncs.kwargs>` for other keyword arguments.\n    72\u2192\n    73\u2192    Examples\n    74\u2192    --------\n    75\u2192    >>> from shapely import Point\n    76\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0))\n    77\u2192    'POINT (0 0)'\n    78\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0), rounding_precision=3, trim=False)\n    79\u2192    'POINT (0.000 0.000)'\n    80\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(0, 0), rounding_precision=-1, trim=False)\n    81\u2192    'POINT (0.0000000000000000 0.0000000000000000)'\n    82\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True)\n    83\u2192    'POINT Z (1 2 3)'\n    84\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True, output_dimension=2)\n    85\u2192    'POINT (1 2)'\n    86\u2192    >>> to_wkt(Point(1, 2, 3), trim=True, old_3d=True)\n    87\u2192    'POINT (1 2 3)'\n    88\u2192\n    89\u2192    Notes\n    90\u2192    -----\n    91\u2192    The defaults differ from the default of the GEOS library. To mimic this,\n    92\u2192    use::\n    93\u2192\n    94\u2192        to_wkt(geometry, rounding_precision=-1, trim=False, output_dimension=2)\n    95\u2192\n    96\u2192    \"\"\"\n    97\u2192    if not np.isscalar(rounding_precision):\n    98\u2192        raise TypeError(\"rounding_precision only accepts scalar values\")\n    99\u2192    if not np.isscalar(trim):\n   100\u2192        raise TypeError(\"trim only accepts scalar values\")\n   101\u2192    if output_dimension is None:\n   102\u2192        output_dimension = 3 if geos_version < (3, 12, 0) else 4\n   103\u2192    elif not np.isscalar(output_dimension):\n   104\u2192        raise TypeError(\"output_dimension only accepts scalar values\")\n   105\u2192    if not np.isscalar(old_3d):\n   106\u2192        raise TypeError(\"old_3d only accepts scalar values\")\n   107\u2192\n   108\u2192    return lib.to_wkt(\n   109\u2192        geometry,\n   110\u2192        np.intc(rounding_precision),\n   111\u2192        np.bool_(trim),\n   112\u2192        np.intc(output_dimension),\n   113\u2192        np.bool_(old_3d),\n   114\u2192        **kwargs,\n   115\u2192    )\n   116\u2192\n   117\u2192\n   118\u2192def to_wkb(\n   119\u2192    geometry,\n   120\u2192    hex=False,\n   121\u2192    output_dimension=None,\n   122\u2192    byte_order=-1,\n   123\u2192    include_srid=False,\n   124\u2192    flavor=\"extended\",\n   125\u2192    **kwargs,\n   126\u2192):\n   127\u2192    r\"\"\"Convert to the Well-Known Binary (WKB) representation of a Geometry.\n   128\u2192\n   129\u2192    The Well-Known Binary format is defined in the `OGC Simple Features\n   130\u2192    Specification for SQL <https://www.opengeospatial.org/standards/sfs>`__.\n   131\u2192\n   132\u2192    The following limitations apply to WKB serialization:\n   133\u2192\n   134\u2192    - linearrings will be converted to linestrings\n   135\u2192    - a point with only NaN coordinates is converted to an empty point\n   136\u2192\n   137\u2192    Parameters\n   138\u2192    ----------\n   139\u2192    geometry : Geometry or array_like\n   140\u2192        Geometry or geometries to convert to WKB.\n   141\u2192    hex : bool, default False\n   142\u2192        If true, export the WKB as a hexadecimal string. The default is to\n   143\u2192        return a binary bytes object.\n   144\u2192    output_dimension : int, default None\n   145\u2192        The output dimension for the WKB. Supported values are 2, 3 and 4 for\n   146\u2192        GEOS 3.12+. Default None will automatically choose 3 or 4, depending on\n   147\u2192        the version of GEOS.\n   148\u2192        Specifying 3 means that up to 3 dimensions will be written but 2D\n   149\u2192        geometries will still be represented as 2D in the WKB representation.\n   150\u2192    byte_order : int, default -1\n   151\u2192        Defaults to native machine byte order (-1). Use 0 to force big endian\n   152\u2192        and 1 for little endian.\n   153\u2192    include_srid : bool, default False\n   154\u2192        If True, the SRID is be included in WKB (this is an extension\n   155\u2192        to the OGC WKB specification). Not allowed when flavor is \"iso\".\n   156\u2192    flavor : {\"iso\", \"extended\"}, default \"extended\"\n   157\u2192        Which flavor of WKB will be returned. The flavor determines how\n   158\u2192        extra dimensionality is encoded with the type number, and whether\n   159\u2192        SRID can be included in the WKB. ISO flavor is \"more standard\" for\n   160\u2192        3D output, and does not support SRID embedding.\n   161\u2192        Both flavors are equivalent when ``output_dimension=2`` (or with